Subject: Scientific validity in metaprogramming experiments

Dave Krieger () writes:
[I'm not sure I should be responding to this since Harry has placed a 
moratorium on it, but since Dave is an ExI director, I assume it is ok.
If not, I will impose self-punishment of 2-days.]

>> None of these points have any scientific validity especially #3. The
>>whole point of the experiment is to make up rituals and test whether
>>they are as efficacious as similiar religious ones. Once a good
>>statistical/objective measure is developed, points #1, #2, and #3 can be
>>dispensed with.  If people who role-play Madonna statistically perform
>>better at sex than those who channel pagan gods of sex, I will have
>>considered the experiment a success.
>
>And if those who invoke Priapus perform better, then is the experiment a
>failure?  The criteria for judging a scientific experiment are not "results
>consistent with hypothesis = success" and "results inconsistent with
>hypothesis = failure".  The experiment is a success if you get either one
>of those results.  A failed experiment is one which ends up saying nothing
>either way about your hypothesis, which is what would happen if you carried
>out this experiment as described by Mark.

   Poor choice of words on my part. I meant that if a proper objective
measure is developed and proper scientific method is used, I would consider
the experiment a success _even if it proved that belief in "gods" worked
better._ I hypothesize that our brains are not genetically built such that
it requires irrational religion to utilitize them fully. This is the 
hypothesis I wish to see tested.

>Ray's confusion comes from the fact that there are two implicit hypotheses
>which the experiment is trying (and failing) to test simultaneously.  They
>are:
>
> A. Rituals are useless for enhancing normal human capabilities
> B. Any ritual or object of concentration is as good as any another
>
  I do not believe we are testing hypothesis A). We want to test
whether channeling gods are any better than role-playing Michael Jordan. I
am not disputing the usefulness of rituals, I am disputing the
usefulness of religious symbols over others. (e.g. I don't believe
they are more special than any others. Whether or not they are
powerful for you depends on your value system and probably your
upbringing.)

>Hypothesis B is basically a summary of Lefty's three objections to Mark's
>experimental design.  Lefty implies that Mark is assuming these conclusions
>to prove them, but I think Mark's experiment is useful except for a fatal
>flaw:  no control group!  The right way to design this experiment is to
>have three experimental groups:

   No, Mark's statements weren't assumptions, they were his hypothesis.
If channeling Wonder Woman gives the same results as channeling religious
symbols, than his hypothesis is proved -- that one symbol is as good as
any other and that religious symbols are no better than teenage mutant
ninja turtles.

   Coming up with the control group is very hard because people have
different value systems. Religious symbols may work better for people who
were brought up on religion, and comic book heros may work better for
those raised on Nitendo.

>Group 1 performs established, traditional rituals in accordance with
>accepted practice.  These rituals would be prescribed and supervised by an
>experienced practitioner.
>
>Group 2 would practice pop-culture rituals such as the ones Mark describes,
>made up out of whole cloth especially for the occasion.  As Mark made clear
>in his post, they should be as slipshod and corny as possible, because we
>want to test the assertion that _any_ ritual will do, right?
>
>Group 3 would practice no ritual, but would approach the efficacy test with
>no special preparation.

  The problem with groups 1 and 2 is that no special attention is given
to the background of those people. If group 2 were made up of pagans
or hermit Christians it would represent a distortion. It still might
be interesting to perform the experiment, especially with group 3.
It may turn out that religious practitioners have no statistical advantage
over the norm and that any perceived improvement is merely the result
of practice. (e.g. subjectively, they feel that they have made great
improvements using religion, but in real terms, they would have achieved
the same thing without the religion.) So, before we perform this
experiment, we should first test the validity of the pagan's claims themselves.

