Subject: EXTROPIAN PRINCIPLES 2.5 (Heavily revised new version)

Michael Price, commenting on Max More's latest Principles:
> Mention uploading?  Okay, I know you mentioned it in the expansion, but
> it seems fairly crucial to long-term immortality.

I agree.  Over the long term uploading plays a dominant
role in life expectency, dwarfing biological life extension, cryonics, etc.  
The self-transformation needed is beyond what most of us are willing to 
even contemplate, overturning some of our most cherished values.  
Cf. my post from a few months ago, "Uploading and Sexual Engineering",
which might be summarized thusly: successful uploads will "worship" 
Athena and Hermes, not Pan.

> Isn't all action dynamic?  Sounds a bit like overkill or hyperbole.

Dynamic optimism is one of the most important and most ignored
of the Principles on this list, IMHO.  An aspect of this I'd
like to see Max More et. al. comment on is the generation and 
nurturing of new ideas.  It's very popular and very easy to go around 
bashing  new ideas.   The old ideas have been well studied, and
the good arguments in their favor have been highly elaborated
(they are "logically deep").  New ideas with the same value or
truth have been far less well studied and have far fewer, less 
sophisticated arguments in their favor.  

It's very easy to bash and trash on new ideas, and we even have
goons who think they are doing somebody a favor by doing 
exclusively this.  One favorite technique Drexler pointed out: it's easy 
to set up strawmen very similar (but not the same) as the design one wants 
to bash, and knock it down, and conclude that the entire scheme is
"unworkable".  I've seen this happen to genetic algorithms ("random
search"), comet mining ("delta-v to Oort cloud is too high"), and
most recently we've seen it here with nanotech.  Not saying that
some of James Donald's criticisms weren't interesting,
just that they were done in a very destructive,
sweeping, de-motivational manner.  A constructive criticism
is not only better informed about the design(s) being criticized, it
also strives to construct superior alternatives instead of just
leaving the opponent a smoking ruin on the battlefield.   
Constructive critics treat new, fragile ideas with more
care than bulworked old paradigms; destructive critics 
knock down anthing that dares pop its head from behind the
walls.

> > 2.  SELF-TRANSFORMATION
> [..] 
> > We practice and plan [...] through means such 
> > [...] General Semantics
> 
> Urrgh.  Please no plugs for GS.

Au contrair.  Many misunderstandings on this list have been
alleviated by use of GS principles (eg the confusion of the
terms "computable" and "information" in the AIT thread) and
many more could have been eliminated.  I consider GS and
related areas of study ("fuzzy" logic, Charles Pierce's
fallibilism, cognitive therapy, etc.) to be a very important
tools of self-transformation, as do many other Extropians,
as does Max More.  Something that would help here is
a popular article/FAQ/tutorial on GS and related topics.
(No, I didn't just volunteer. :-)

Here's a compromise: if we eliminate GS,
we should also get rid of Ayn Rand. (asbsestos suit on! :-)

> >, fuzzy logic
> 
> An abused and overused term.  I suggest you drop it.

Alas, that's what the dominant form of non-Aristotlean
logic calls itself; we don't have much choice unless we 
want to invent our own private language.

> How do the terms post-human [after-human?] and trans-human [beyond-
> human?] differ? 

One way to think of transhuman is the era between cryonic
reanimation and uploading.  Another is major improvements
over the human norm short of uploading (neural transplant,
smart drugs, imbedded chips, etc.).   I think of posthumans
as uploads making backup copies, grokking the universe in
all spectra, cogitating with Jupiter-sized brains, etc.
I don't think there's any fixed definition.

