Date: Thu, 15 Jul 1993 19:36:16 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: HEx: New Exchange Regulations

The liquidity problem is primarily caused by the fact that new issuers
are greatly overpricing their shares (perhaps in initital imitation
of HEX's own 100/share price, which is not free market set and
is irrelevent to the other prices).  Derek Zahn pointed this
out well.  More fundamentally, we have these three problems:

(a) New issues are unlimited.
(b) Share splits are unlimated.
(c) Thorne supply is fixed.

Rowan's 30% auction plan deals with (a), but not (b) or (c).
There is a ceiling on market prices set by Thornes (this is
flexible depending on money velocity, but not infinite), but
no floor.  Right now, several issues are selling for over
1,000 times what they're worth.  I won't name names, but
obviously people are manipulating their own or each other's
reputation prices, oustanding value, or whatever else is perceived
as the "reputation rating" to bizarre extremes.  I'm not sure there 
are any shares that are underpriced.

If Thorne supply is allowed to grow, it should be done in
some formulaic, not arbitrary manner.  For example, Thorne
supply might be increased every time there is a share split
or IPO, by the increase in total shares in the market
times average price per share.

As it stands now, the current prices reflect almost entirely
price-fixing skill, and have practically nothing to do with
reputation.  Besides, is the "reputation rating" supposed to
be price per share, oustanding market value, total market
value, or what?   (Of course, I vote for share price. :-)
Very strange and interesting and fun, but don't take it too seriously, 
folks.

