If my paragraph was unclear, I will restate in a more
lucid fashion.

Your argument that irregular objects can be engineered to
slide freely over each other assumes that the spatial
frequencies of the van der Waals fields of both objects
can be kept incommensurate.  This is true if the spatial
frequencies of the "force fields" of both objects only
varies on the same scale as atoms.

Because van der Waals forces are not strictly pairwise
additive, the Fourier transform of the "force field" will
contain spatial frequencies that correspond to the sum and
difference frequencies of the Fourier transform of the
distribution of the atoms.  In simpler language: A mess.

Thus if the object is irregular, its "force field" will
necessarily contain low spatial frequency components, even
though the distribution of atoms can be engineered to
contain no low spatial frequency components.  This is
analogous to the moire patterns in HOPG which are caused
by the beats between layers with different orientations,
but will be less regular.  The range, strength, and
character of these forces will vary independently.  For
example the van der Waals forces of highly polarizable
groups of atoms, are relatively long range, but primarily
affect other highly polarizable groups of atoms.  Van der
Waals forces are not a single force, but rather a crude
averaging of many separate forms of complex interaction.

If you could freely control the exact position of atoms
with respect to each other then you would have enough
degrees of freedom to engineer this problem away.  But you
do not.  You can have one kind of bond, or another kind of
bond.  You can have one arrangement, or a grossly different
arrangement.

To make this clear, consider a particular case:  Suppose
you have two sliding components, each of a thousand atoms
or so.  Suppose we wish to ensure that the potential is a
constant function of position.

Suppose we adjust the potential to be equal at one hundred
points, giving us one hundred constraints.  This is fairly
severe, yet it is clearly not sufficient to permit sliding.
To ensure sliding we have to make the potential equal on
a set of points that is dense compared to the interatomic
spacing, since we know that high spatial frequency
components of the potential must be small, but the
frequency components that correspond to the interatomic
spacing and smaller frequencies will be will be quite large
(proportional to the square root of the number of atoms, if
we ignore high order effects), unless we specifically make
adjustments to balance them out, as Drexler proposes to do.

In a designed surface we by design specifically adjust some
design parameters to minimize the variation at each
particular spatial frequency.

So for large rigid irregular objects (large enough for the
total van der Waals binding energy to be vastly greater
than kT, small enough that the surfaces are in intimate
contact everywhere) we find that the number of design
parameters constraining us is larger than the size in
atoms of the design.  Furthermore the constraints have to
be adjusted to meet a continuous degree of freedom whereas
our adjustment in the position of the atoms is discrete,
thus one constraint parameter will use up many choices in
the positions of atoms.  We have an over constrained problem
with non linear constraints. Such problems are always hard
to solve, and usually have no solutions, except for special
cases such as strictly regular symmetric solutions.

If our components are small enough that we can approximate
them as low dimensional then the number of constraints must
grow faster than the spatial frequency of irregularities,
times the size of the object.  But the number of degrees of
freedom available to adjust in order to meet these degrees
of freedom grows only as fast as the number of the
irregularities.

Thus a sufficiently large irregular sliding nano structure
must impossible.  How big is sufficiently large?  Very
hard to say. I really have no idea.  The behavior of large
molecules, and the behavior of proteins, does not give good
grounds for optimism.

Suppose however that we are only interested in motion in
one direction, which is the usual case, and our structure
is large enough that several irregularities (design
choices) exist perpendicular to the direction of motion.
Then as we scale up, our surface becomes two dimensional
and our structure a three dimensional solid, rather than a
collection of individual atoms. and my argument about
counting parameters no longer applies because the number of
degrees of freedom capable of being engineered increases as
the square of the size, whereas the number of constraints
increases some what faster than the size. But now we are
talking about small macroscopic components with nanoscale
engineered surfaces, rather than molecular components.

Our rods are now small diamonds, rather than carbyne
molecules, our gears will look like gears, not like crown
ethers molecules.

Consideration of your arguments has convinced me that
"nano" technology can work for rigid clockwork machines
that are sufficiently macro.  The components are much
larger than active structures involved in DNA transcription
and the like.

A major problem in designing an irregular non stick surface
is that we do not accurately know the relationship between
the discrete parameters we can control adjust (atom
position), and the parameters that we want to control
(potential as a function of position).  For a large
irregular surface being fine tuned the relationship will
be complex and indirect.

Of course we do not have to get it perfect.  As you point
out we can just use a bigger hammer (motor).  But then of
course we run the risk of lock up.  But lock up can be
solved by making the apparatus a bit larger still.

In our bodies, proteins directly manipulate molecules that
are the same size as large amino acids, and RNA catalysts
directly manipulate somewhat larger objects.

To manipulate smaller molecules or atoms, they usually hook
it to a purine molecule, often adenosine, and then
manipulate the complex, thus purine molecules are used as
tweezers to deal with smaller molecules.

A "nanomachine" made out of diamond like materials with an
non stick surface would need to have components that are
too large, so that atoms and ordinary molecules of would be
inconveniently small for it to handle directly.  It would
need to manipulate atoms with something very like a small
atomic force microscope, much as we do today.  It's probe
would be much larger than an adenosine molecule.

Such a design violates no laws of physics and would
certainly work, assuming we could figure out exactly where
to put a very large number of atoms so as to avoid static
friction.  But this is not what most people envisage as
nanotechnology.

I think that your reply to my criticisms of nanotechnology
based on dry bearings, rigidity, and determinism, can be
fairly summarized as follows: "That there are no problems
with dry bearings and rigid structures that cannot be
solved by making the components bigger and using a vast
amount of trial and error design every time we change
something."

Have I made myself clear this time?

